{:edb/db-before-t 2591 :edb/db-after-t 2592 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535904 50 #inst "2026-10-15T05:58:53.757Z" 13194139535904 17592186045443 true] [17592186229183 1038 #edb/ref 17592186539398 13194139535904 17592186045443 true] [17592186229183 1038 #edb/ref 17592186539401 13194139535904 17592186045443 true] [17592186229183 1038 #edb/ref 17592186539408 13194139535904 17592186045443 true] [17592186229183 1038 #edb/ref 17592186539423 13194139535904 17592186045443 true] [17592186229183 1038 #edb/ref 17592186539425 13194139535904 17592186045443 true] [17592186229183 1038 #edb/ref 17592186539438 13194139535904 17592186045443 true] [17592186229183 1038 #edb/ref 17592186539444 13194139535904 17592186045443 true] [17592186229183 1038 #edb/ref 17592186539451 13194139535904 17592186045443 true] [17592186229183 1038 #edb/ref 17592186539460 13194139535904 17592186045443 true] [17592186229183 1038 #edb/ref 17592186539462 13194139535904 17592186045443 true] [17592186229183 1040 #edb/ref 17592186539463 13194139535904 17592186045443 true] [17592186539393 1080 "316.3\\,\\text{lb}" 13194139535904 17592186045443 true] [17592186539393 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535904 17592186045443 true] [17592186539393 1083 #uuid "09aa09e5-f47d-5621-9398-dc8978b0d87a" 13194139535904 17592186045443 true] [17592186539394 1080 "89.3\\,\\text{lb}" 13194139535904 17592186045443 true] [17592186539394 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535904 17592186045443 true] [17592186539394 1083 #uuid "10b62b53-1b76-5884-8cf6-e623e6a2f279" 13194139535904 17592186045443 true] [17592186539395 1080 "386.0\\,\\text{lb}" 13194139535904 17592186045443 true] [17592186539395 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535904 17592186045443 true] [17592186539395 1083 #uuid "17d1e1a0-3c64-5ef9-9dbe-759c9e661398" 13194139535904 17592186045443 true] [17592186539396 1080 "233.0\\,\\text{lb}" 13194139535904 17592186045443 true] [17592186539396 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535904 17592186045443 true] [17592186539396 1083 #uuid "19329e8b-f3ba-516f-af71-d4b1d311b395" 13194139535904 17592186045443 true] [17592186539397 1080 "288.9\\,\\text{lb}" 13194139535904 17592186045443 true] [17592186539397 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535904 17592186045443 true] [17592186539397 1083 #uuid "195e6da9-9d93-5a3f-abbd-e60df2db40ad" 13194139535904 17592186045443 true] [17592186539398 1068 #edb/ref 17592186539442 13194139535904 17592186045443 true] [17592186539398 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535904 17592186045443 true] [17592186539398 1071 "A disinfectant reservoir in a water treatment facility can hold up to $180\\,\\text{gal}$ of liquid. Initially, it contains $60\\,\\text{gal}$ of disinfectant solution with $10\\,\\text{lb}$ of dissolved disinfectant. A more concentrated solution with concentration $0.5\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ flows into the reservoir at a rate of $25\\,\\text{gal/min},$ while the completely mixed solution drains out at a rate of $20\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ Rounded to one decimal place, find the amount of disinfectant in the reservoir at the moment it begins to overflow." 13194139535904 17592186045443 true] [17592186539398 1072 #uuid "1a0728d4-a0d3-515f-805e-eb1a7d3c019c" 13194139535904 17592186045443 true] [17592186539398 1074 "Since $Q$ is the amount of disinfectant in the reservoir at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which disinfectant enters the reservoir minus the rate at which disinfectant leaves the reservoir.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{lb}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which disinfectant enters the reservoir.\n\nFirst, note that a solution containing $0.5\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ of disinfectant enters the reservoir at a rate of $25\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *disinfectant* enters the reservoir is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.5\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (25\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.5\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (25\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =12.5\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which disinfectant exits the reservoir.\n\nTo find the rate at which disinfectant leaves the reservoir, we must first determine the volume of solution in the reservoir at time $t.$\n\nThe reservoir initially contains $60\\,\\text{gal}$ of solution; solution enters at $25\\,\\text{gal/min}$ and mixed solution leaves at $20\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of solution in the reservoir after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\nV(t)=60+25t-20t=5(12+t)\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the reservoir is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of disinfectant in the reservoir, the concentration of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{5(12+t)}\\,\\text{lb/gal}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $20\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *disinfectant* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(20\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{5(12+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(20\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{5(12+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{20Q}{5(12+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4Q}{12+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =12.5-\\frac{4Q}{12+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{4}{12+t}\\,Q=12.5.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 4/(12+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|12+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(12+t)^{4}} \\\\  & =(12+t)^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(12+t)^{4}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(12+t)^{4}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{4}{12+t}\\,Q) & =(12+t)^{4}\\cdot 12.5 \\\\ (12+t)^{4}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+4(12+t)^{3}Q & =12.5(12+t)^{4} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(12+t)^{4}) & =12.5(12+t)^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(12+t)^{4})\\,dt & =\\int 12.5(12+t)^{4}\\,dt \\\\ Q(12+t)^{4} & =12.5\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}(12+t)^{5}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =2.5(12+t)+\\frac{C}{(12+t)^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the reservoir initially contains $10\\,\\text{lb}$ of disinfectant. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=10,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =10 \\\\ 2.5(12+0)+\\frac{C}{(12+0)^{4}} & =10 \\\\ 30+\\frac{C}{(12)^{4}} & =10 \\\\ \\frac{C}{{12}^{4}} & =-20 \\\\ C & =-20(12)^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =2.5(12+t)-\\frac{20(12)^{4}}{(12+t)^{4}} \\\\  & =2.5(12+t)-20{(\\frac{12}{12+t})}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the reservoir begins to overflow. The reservoir has a capacity of $180\\,\\text{gal},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=180$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =180 \\\\ 60+25{t}_{o}-20{t}_{o} & =180 \\\\ 60+5{t}_{o} & =180 \\\\ 5{t}_{o} & =120 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =24\\,\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, rounded to one decimal place, the amount of disinfectant in the reservoir at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(24) & =2.5(12+24)-20{(\\frac{12}{12+24})}^{4} \\\\  & =90-20{(\\frac{12}{36})}^{4} \\\\  & ≈89.8\\,\\text{lb}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535904 17592186045443 true] [17592186539399 1080 "30.5\\,\\text{lb}" 13194139535904 17592186045443 true] [17592186539399 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535904 17592186045443 true] [17592186539399 1083 #uuid "1accc569-7a13-58f0-bb4e-fe4d837666e7" 13194139535904 17592186045443 true] [17592186539400 1080 "89.8\\,\\text{lb}" 13194139535904 17592186045443 true] [17592186539400 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535904 17592186045443 true] [17592186539400 1083 #uuid "1d6d3790-da14-581b-85b9-f63924341cef" 13194139535904 17592186045443 true] [17592186539401 1068 #edb/ref 17592186539417 13194139535904 17592186045443 true] [17592186539401 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535904 17592186045443 true] [17592186539401 1071 "A disinfectant reservoir in a water treatment facility can hold up to $368\\,\\text{gal}$ of liquid. Initially, it contains $300\\,\\text{gal}$ of disinfectant solution with $160\\,\\text{lb}$ of dissolved disinfectant. A more concentrated solution with concentration $0.8\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ flows into the reservoir at a rate of $20\\,\\text{gal/min},$ while the completely mixed solution drains out at a rate of $12\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ Rounded to one decimal place, find the amount of disinfectant in the reservoir at the moment it begins to overflow." 13194139535904 17592186045443 true] [17592186539401 1072 #uuid "21bb65fc-94da-5d56-9b2a-2fe8f762fdb9" 13194139535904 17592186045443 true] [17592186539401 1074 "Since $Q$ is the amount of disinfectant in the reservoir at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which disinfectant enters the reservoir minus the rate at which disinfectant leaves the reservoir.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{lb}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which disinfectant enters the reservoir.\n\nFirst, note that a solution containing $0.8\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ of disinfectant enters the reservoir at a rate of $20\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *disinfectant* enters the reservoir is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.8\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (20\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.8\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (20\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =16\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which disinfectant exits the reservoir.\n\nTo find the rate at which disinfectant leaves the reservoir, we must first determine the volume of solution in the reservoir at time $t.$\n\nThe reservoir initially contains $300\\,\\text{gal}$ of solution; solution enters at $20\\,\\text{gal/min}$ and mixed solution leaves at $12\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of solution in the reservoir after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\nV(t)=300+20t-12t=8(37.5+t)\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the reservoir is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of disinfectant in the reservoir, the concentration of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{8(37.5+t)}\\,\\text{lb/gal}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $12\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *disinfectant* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(12\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{8(37.5+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(12\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{8(37.5+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{12Q}{8(37.5+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1.5Q}{37.5+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =16-\\frac{1.5Q}{37.5+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1.5}{37.5+t}\\,Q=16.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 1.5/(37.5+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{1.5\\operatorname{ln}⁡|37.5+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(37.5+t)^{1.5}} \\\\  & =(37.5+t)^{1.5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(37.5+t)^{1.5}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(37.5+t)^{1.5}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1.5}{37.5+t}\\,Q) & =(37.5+t)^{1.5}\\cdot 16 \\\\ (37.5+t)^{1.5}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+1.5(37.5+t)^{0.5}Q & =16(37.5+t)^{1.5} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(37.5+t)^{1.5}) & =16(37.5+t)^{1.5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(37.5+t)^{1.5})\\,dt & =\\int 16(37.5+t)^{1.5}\\,dt \\\\ Q(37.5+t)^{1.5} & =16\\cdot \\frac{1}{2.5}(37.5+t)^{2.5}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =6.4(37.5+t)+\\frac{C}{(37.5+t)^{1.5}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the reservoir initially contains $160\\,\\text{lb}$ of disinfectant. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=160,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =160 \\\\ 6.4(37.5+0)+\\frac{C}{(37.5+0)^{1.5}} & =160 \\\\ 240+\\frac{C}{(37.5)^{1.5}} & =160 \\\\ \\frac{C}{{37.5}^{1.5}} & =-80 \\\\ C & =-80(37.5)^{1.5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =6.4(37.5+t)-\\frac{80(37.5)^{1.5}}{(37.5+t)^{1.5}} \\\\  & =6.4(37.5+t)-80{(\\frac{37.5}{37.5+t})}^{1.5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the reservoir begins to overflow. The reservoir has a capacity of $368\\,\\text{gal},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=368$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =368 \\\\ 300+20{t}_{o}-12{t}_{o} & =368 \\\\ 300+8{t}_{o} & =368 \\\\ 8{t}_{o} & =68 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =8.5\\,\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, rounded to one decimal place, the amount of disinfectant in the reservoir at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(8.5) & =6.4(37.5+8.5)-80{(\\frac{37.5}{37.5+8.5})}^{1.5} \\\\  & =294.4-80{(\\frac{37.5}{46})}^{1.5} \\\\  & ≈235.5\\,\\text{lb}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535904 17592186045443 true] [17592186539402 1075 #edb/ref 17592186539411 13194139535904 17592186045443 true] [17592186539402 1075 #edb/ref 17592186539424 13194139535904 17592186045443 true] [17592186539402 1075 #edb/ref 17592186539434 13194139535904 17592186045443 true] [17592186539402 1075 #edb/ref 17592186539437 13194139535904 17592186045443 true] [17592186539402 1075 #edb/ref 17592186539447 13194139535904 17592186045443 true] [17592186539402 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139535904 17592186045443 true] [17592186539402 1077 "selection" 13194139535904 17592186045443 true] [17592186539402 1078 #edb/ref 17592186539447 13194139535904 17592186045443 true] [17592186539402 1079 #uuid "22f5cfc2-660c-58a7-af4f-432fa13dacdf" 13194139535904 17592186045443 true] [17592186539403 1080 "284.9\\,\\text{lb}" 13194139535904 17592186045443 true] [17592186539403 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535904 17592186045443 true] [17592186539403 1083 #uuid "2bf04547-57c3-53c4-840a-33e618532e81" 13194139535904 17592186045443 true] [17592186539404 1080 "287.0\\,\\text{lb}" 13194139535904 17592186045443 true] [17592186539404 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535904 17592186045443 true] [17592186539404 1083 #uuid "3070ff1a-7364-514c-8463-27955639a9a3" 13194139535904 17592186045443 true] [17592186539405 1080 "226.4\\,\\text{lb}" 1319