+t)\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the tank is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of dye in the tank, the concentration of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{2(16.5+t)}\\,\\text{lb/gal}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $1\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *dye* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(1\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{2(16.5+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(1\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{2(16.5+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1Q}{2(16.5+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.5Q}{16.5+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =1.5-\\frac{0.5Q}{16.5+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{0.5}{16.5+t}\\,Q=1.5.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 0.5/(16.5+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{0.5\\operatorname{ln}⁡|16.5+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(16.5+t)^{0.5}} \\\\  & =(16.5+t)^{0.5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(16.5+t)^{0.5}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(16.5+t)^{0.5}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{0.5}{16.5+t}\\,Q) & =(16.5+t)^{0.5}\\cdot 1.5 \\\\ (16.5+t)^{0.5}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+0.5(16.5+t)^{-0.5}Q & =1.5(16.5+t)^{0.5} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(16.5+t)^{0.5}) & =1.5(16.5+t)^{0.5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(16.5+t)^{0.5})\\,dt & =\\int 1.5(16.5+t)^{0.5}\\,dt \\