t, the amount of solution in the reservoir after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\nV(t)=315+15t-5t=10(31.5+t)\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the reservoir is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of salt in the reservoir, the concentration of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{10(31.5+t)}\\,\\text{lb/gal}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $5\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *salt* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(5\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{10(31.5+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(5\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{10(31.5+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5Q}{10(31.5+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.5Q}{31.5+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =18-\\frac{0.5Q}{31.5+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{0.5}{31.5+t}\\,Q=18.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 0.5/(31.5+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{0.5\\operatorname{ln}⁡|31.5+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(31.5+t)^{0.5}} \\\\  & =(31.5+t)^{0.5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(31.5+t)^{0.5}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(31.5+t)^{0.5}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{0.5}{31.5+t}\\,Q) & =(31.5+t)^{0.5}\\cdot 18 \\\\ (31.5+t)^{0.5}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+0.5(31.5+t)^{-0.5}Q & =18(31.5+t)^{0.5} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(31.5+t)^{0.5}) & =18(31.5+t)^{0.5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(31.5+t)^{0.5})\\,dt & =\\int 18(31.5+t)^{0.5}\\,dt \\\\ Q(31.5+t)^{0.5} & =18\\cdot \\frac{1}{1.5}(31.5+t)^{1.5}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =12(31.5+t)+\\frac{C}{(31.5+t)^{0.5}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the reservoir initially contains $126\\,\\text{lb}$ of salt. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=126,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =126 \\\\ 12(31.5+0)+\\frac{C}{(31.5+0)^{0.5}} & =126 \\\\ 378+\\frac{C}{(31.5)^{0.5}} & =126 \\\\ \\frac{C}{(31.5)^{0.5}} & =-252 \\\\ C & =-252(31.5)^{0.5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =12(31.5+t)-\\frac{252(31.5)^{0.5}}{(31.5+t)^{0.5}} \\\\  & =12(31.5+t)-252{(\\frac{31.5}{31.5+t})}^{0.5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the reservoir begins to overflow. The reservoir has a capacity of $490\\,\\text{gal},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=490$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =490 \\\\ 315+15{t}_{o}-5{t}_{o} & =490 \\\\ 315+10{t}_{o} & =490 \\\\ 10{t}_{o} & =175 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =17.5\\,\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, rounded to one decimal place, the amount of salt in the reservoir at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(17.5) & =12(31.5+17.5)-252{(\\frac{31.5}{31.5+17.5})}^{0.5} \\\\  & =588-252{(\\frac{31.5}{49})}^{0.5} \\\\  & ≈386.0\\,\\text{lb}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535904 17592186045443 true] [17592186539463 1168 "generate/scratch/17592186229183.py" 13194139535904 17592186045443 true] [17592186539463 1169 #uuid "7e061520-71a8-55d4-a2eb-a7cef8163589" 13194139535904 17592186045443 true]] :edb/tempids {"09aa09e5-f47d-5621-9398-dc8978b0d87a" 17592186539393 "10b62b53-1b76-5884-8cf6-e623e6a2f279" 17592186539394 "17d1e1a0-3c64-5ef9-9dbe-759c9e661398" 17592186539395 "19329e8b-f3ba-516f-af71-d4b1d311b395" 17592186539396 "195e6da9-9d93-5a3f-abbd-e60df2db40ad" 17592186539397 "1a0728d